00:31:07,050 --> 00:31:12,860
Let's start with some essential vocabulary that you'll probably encounter in, well, almost any professional setting.

550
00:31:13,440 --> 00:31:15,910
And this isn't just about knowing definitions, is it?

551
00:31:16,130 --> 00:31:16,990
No, not at all.

552
00:31:17,070 --> 00:31:26,760
It's about understanding the kind of subtle implications these words have in the workplace and how using them effectively really demonstrates your command of business English.

553
00:31:26,900 --> 00:31:27,120
Right.

554
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:27,640
Yeah.

555
00:31:27,680 --> 00:31:33,260
These are words that help frame expectations, define responsibilities, and articulate your capabilities.

556
00:31:33,680 --> 00:31:37,910
Let's start with one that honestly underpins pretty much all project management.

557
00:31:38,250 --> 00:31:38,650
Deadline.

558
00:31:38,770 --> 00:31:39,190
Deadline.

559
00:31:39,550 --> 00:31:39,890
Okay.

560
00:31:40,470 --> 00:31:44,310
So what exactly does deadline signify in a professional context?

561
00:31:44,350 --> 00:31:45,470
Is it just the end date?

562
00:31:45,560 --> 00:31:46,700
It's more than just an end date.

563
00:31:46,720 --> 00:31:47,580
Yeah, precisely.

564
00:31:47,860 --> 00:31:53,930
A deadline is the final time or date by which a task or maybe a whole project must be completed.

565
00:31:54,190 --> 00:31:56,160
It's like the ultimate finish line for your work.

566
00:31:56,460 --> 00:31:58,120
It ensures everything stays on schedule.

567
00:31:58,260 --> 00:31:59,500
And missing it is bad news.

568
00:32:00,010 --> 00:32:01,070
Oh, definitely.

569
00:32:01,050 --> 00:32:07,380
Missing it can cause this whole ripple effect, right, leading to costly delays, sometimes even project failure.

570
00:32:07,800 --> 00:32:12,940
So, for instance, if your manager says the report is due Friday at 5 p.m., that's it.

571
00:32:13,040 --> 00:32:14,470
That's an absolute cutoff.

572
00:32:15,250 --> 00:32:21,760
Understanding and respecting deadlines is just paramount for productivity, reliability, really in any role.

573
00:32:22,140 --> 00:32:23,620
And you'll notice how crucial this is, right?

574
00:32:23,660 --> 00:32:29,300
Not just for getting things done, but for showing you're reliable, that you understand how projects flow.

575
00:32:30,020 --> 00:32:32,180
Okay, moving on, let's unpack strengths.

576
00:32:32,790 --> 00:32:35,070
How do we effectively talk about our strengths?

577
00:32:35,180 --> 00:32:35,550
Right.

578
00:32:35,730 --> 00:32:41,590
So strengths refer to your positive qualities or skills, basically the areas where you naturally excel.

579
00:32:41,850 --> 00:32:46,800
When an interviewer asks about your strengths, what they're really looking for is the unique value you bring to the table.

580
00:32:47,360 --> 00:32:53,490
So, for example, if you're really good with numbers, processing complex data, your strength might be analytical thinking.

581
00:32:53,510 --> 00:32:59,860
Or if you can, you know, effortlessly convey ideas and you're a great listener, then strong communication is a clear strength.

582
00:32:59,840 --> 00:33:02,400
But you can't just say, I'm a strong communicator, right?

583
00:33:02,420 --> 00:33:03,330
Exactly.

584
00:33:03,530 --> 00:33:04,110
That's the key.

585
00:33:04,130 --> 00:33:07,590
You have to be specific and back these claims up with tangible examples.

586
00:33:08,250 --> 00:33:13,260
You need to illustrate how your strengths have actually led to successful outcomes in past situations.

587
00:33:13,600 --> 00:33:15,850
It's really about showing, not just telling.

588
00:33:16,090 --> 00:33:18,330
That's where the power of specific examples comes in.

589
00:33:18,990 --> 00:33:23,740
Totally transforms a generic claim into like actual evidence.

590
00:33:24,190 --> 00:33:27,070
Okay, so on the flip side, we have weaknesses.

591
00:33:27,850 --> 00:33:29,800
This question often catches people off guard, doesn't it?

592
00:33:29,840 --> 00:33:31,200
What's the best way to approach it?

593
00:33:31,220 --> 00:33:32,280
Yeah, it definitely can.

594
00:33:32,660 --> 00:33:38,350
Weaknesses are essentially areas where you acknowledge you might need some improvement or development.

595
00:33:38,370 --> 00:33:41,970
Now, this isn't about confessing some fatal flaw that's going to get you kicked out the door.

596
00:33:42,410 --> 00:33:42,610
Right.

597
00:33:42,630 --> 00:33:47,170
It's really about demonstrating self-awareness and, crucially, a growth mindset.

598
00:33:47,280 --> 00:33:48,780
Employers really value that.

599
00:33:49,200 --> 00:33:54,160
So, for instance, you might struggle a bit with, say, public speaking or maybe over-perfectionism.

600
00:33:54,140 --> 00:33:58,390
But the crucial part, and this is really important, isn't just identifying the weakness.

601
00:33:59,030 --> 00:34:02,670
It's outlining the concrete steps you're actively taking to address it.

602
00:34:03,070 --> 00:34:06,020
So have you joined a public speaking group?

603
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:08,560
Do you set time limits for yourself on tasks?

604
00:34:09,100 --> 00:34:11,440
Do you proactively ask for feedback now?

605
00:34:12,180 --> 00:34:21,640
Showing that journey, that improvement process, is way more impactful than claiming you have no weaknesses or worse, giving that tired cliche like, oh, I just work too hard.

606
00:34:21,620 --> 00:34:24,380
Yeah, I'm too much of a perfectionist line.

607
00:34:24,420 --> 00:34:26,250
It can sound a bit disingenuous sometimes.

608
00:34:26,270 --> 00:34:26,950
It really can.

609
00:34:27,010 --> 00:34:29,260
But showing you're working on it, that's powerful.

610
00:34:29,420 --> 00:34:36,660
It shows you're committed to personal and professional growth, which signals you'll likely be a valuable, evolving employee.

611
00:34:36,640 --> 00:34:40,360
OK, our final foundational term for this section, onboarding.

612
00:34:40,980 --> 00:34:44,350
What does a new hire typically experience during this onboarding period?

613
00:34:44,410 --> 00:34:44,910
Good question.

614
00:34:44,930 --> 00:34:46,270
What is onboarding exactly?

615
00:34:46,370 --> 00:34:51,020
So onboarding is basically the structured process of integrating a new employee into a company.

616
00:34:51,440 --> 00:34:55,640
It's designed to make you feel comfortable, informed and prepared for your role.

617
00:34:55,660 --> 00:34:57,010
So it's more than just paperwork.

618
00:34:57,260 --> 00:34:57,960
Oh, much more.

619
00:34:58,360 --> 00:35:10,500
It typically includes understanding company policies, the culture, getting training on specific tools and tasks you'll be using, meeting your new team, and importantly, learning who to go to for support or questions.

620
00:35:11,140 --> 00:35:17,040
Really effective onboarding sets the stage for a new hire's success and helps build that sense of belonging right from day one.

621
00:35:17,020 --> 00:35:17,760
Makes sense.

622
00:35:17,780 --> 00:35:30,070
Okay, so with that essential vocabulary kind of firmly in mind, let's talk about a really practical framework, something that will elevate your interview responses, especially for those tricky behavioral or situational questions.

623
00:35:30,150 --> 00:35:31,470
It's called the STAR method.

624
00:35:31,930 --> 00:35:33,020
Ah, yes, STAR.

625
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:36,240
It stands for Situation, Task, Action, and Result.

626
00:35:36,800 --> 00:35:45,650
It's such a powerful narrative tool because it lets you tell a concise but really compelling story that provides concrete evidence of your skills, your problem-solving abilities.

627
00:35:46,090 --> 00:35:49,710
It basically transforms abstract claims into tangible experiences.

628
00:35:50,050 --> 00:35:50,930
Okay, let's break that down.

629
00:35:51,090 --> 00:35:51,760
S-T-A-R.

630
00:35:51,890 --> 00:35:52,940
So, situation.

631
00:35:53,020 --> 00:35:54,180
First, you set the scene, right?

632
00:35:54,460 --> 00:35:56,280
Give the necessary context for your story.

633
00:35:56,620 --> 00:35:58,260
What was the specific scenario you were in?

634
00:35:58,640 --> 00:36:00,220
Then, task.

635
00:36:00,240 --> 00:36:00,740
Next...

636
00:36:00,860 --> 00:36:04,620
Describe the specific goal you needed to achieve or maybe the challenge you were facing.

637
00:36:04,900 --> 00:36:07,130
What was actually expected of you in that situation?

638
00:36:07,770 --> 00:36:08,490
Then action.

639
00:36:09,170 --> 00:36:10,230
This is the core, right?

640
00:36:10,270 --> 00:36:12,510
This is where you detail the specific steps you took.

641
00:36:12,890 --> 00:36:17,440
What did you personally do to address the situation or complete the task?

642
00:36:17,900 --> 00:36:19,200
Got to focus on your contributions.

643
00:36:19,260 --> 00:36:19,520
Yes.

644
00:36:19,700 --> 00:36:21,340
The you part is critical there.

645
00:36:21,590 --> 00:36:22,870
And finally, result.

646
00:36:23,230 --> 00:36:23,590
Yeah.

647
00:36:23,610 --> 00:36:25,090
Explain the outcome of your actions.

648
00:36:25,330 --> 00:36:26,610
What was the positive impact?

649
00:36:26,870 --> 00:36:28,760
What did you learn from the whole experience?

650
00:36:28,840 --> 00:36:37,230
This method is just incredibly effective because it moves you way beyond vague statements like I'm a good team player to a clear evidence based narrative.

651
00:36:37,610 --> 00:36:45,400
You'll actually hear Leonard in our tech simulation coming up naturally using this method when he describes that tricky e-commerce project issue.

652
00:36:45,860 --> 00:36:51,660
It really showcases how seamlessly it can be applied in practice and how much it elevates your professional communication.

653
00:36:52,070 --> 00:36:52,200
Right.

654
00:36:52,220 --> 00:36:54,930
It makes the answer structured but also compelling.

